ion issues, but we already know no concrete examples. Could ask about other panel members' observations on B's crew cooperation. But we have no tool to ask now? We could ask via tool to James or Emily, but we are Sarah. We could ask a tool to James or Emily for more info. However the instruction says we can ask tool to other agents. We could ask James for any concrete examples of B's communication issues, but we already asked Sarah and got none. Could ask James for any additional info on B's leadership or decision quality. But we might want to move discussion forward.\n\nGiven the current state, we have covered A and D concerns, B's strengths, C's strengths and concerns. We still need to discuss decision quality for B and D, technical competence for A and D, etc. But we need to keep each turn focused.\n\nPerhaps Sarah can now bring up a point about Candidate C's adaptability: not willing to further education, which is a concern for long-distance pilots who need ongoing training. This could strengthen B's case. Or discuss that C's egocentric attitude may affect crew cooperation more than B's pretentiousness.\n\nThus Sarah can say: I remain with Candidate B, because while C is reliable and creates positive atmosphere, his unwillingness to further education and egocentric nature pose higher risk for adaptability and crew cooperation than B's manageable communication issues.\n\nMake sure to reference prior discussion: Emily's note about lack of concrete examples, and James's earlier points about A and D concerns.\n\nThus produce public message: \"I agree with Emily; I still recommend B because ...\"\n\nVote B.\n\nProceed."}
